    },
    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Mao',
            subtitle: 'Card Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Mao is a unique card game known for its extreme secrecy and metagame elements. It is the most famous of the 'secret rule' games, where the only rule that may be explained to a new player is: 'The rules of Mao cannot be told.' New players must learn the rules through trial and error, as they are penalized (by drawing cards) for every infraction. Each winner of a round also gets to create a new secret rule for the next round, meaning the game evolves indefinitely.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Its exact origin is shrouded in mystery, intentional or otherwise. It appeared in various US university campuses in the 1960s and 70s.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "3-8 players. One player is the 'Chairman' (dealer/moderator).",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Two shuffled standard decks. All cards face down.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Two shuffled standard decks. All cards face down.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Each player is dealt 7 cards. Players CANNOT look at their cards until the game officially starts.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: "Dutch Mao: Each player invents one rule at the start; no standard matching rules. Also known as The People's Democratic Dictatorship.",
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Hand size: Initial hand is three cards in some variants, seven in others.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Winner adds rule: Winner of each round adds one secret rule for the next round; often only dealer knows it.',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
